// SOLDER at a hosted terminal -- the console the language is meant to be met through, on a machine
// where it can be tried without a board in the way.
//
// **Everything interesting is in the package.** The read-run-print loop is `sh.sysl.solder.session`,
// because there is going to be more than one console -- this, and a board over a serial cable -- and
// a loop written out per platform is copies that drift. What is left here is the only
// platform-dependent part: which streams the lines come from.
//
// `host` rather than `desktop` or `mac` because that is sysl's own word for it -- `Target.host`, and
// "a hosted program" throughout the library. This builds anywhere hosted.

import sh.sysl.solder.*
import sysl.io.{stdin, console_lines}
import sysl.term.edit.editor
import sysl.posix.tty.{raw, cooked, tty_writer}

// Which console this is, which is the only platform-dependent code here.
//
// **`raw` failing is not an error -- it is the other situation.** At a terminal it puts the kernel out
// of the way so the editor can see a keystroke as it is typed; with input redirected from a file or a
// pipe there is no terminal to change, and then the editor is the wrong facility entirely: nothing is
// being typed, nothing should be echoed, and `console_lines` is what reads a stream of lines.
//
// So `solder-host < script.solder` still works, and works *better* than it would through an editor.
var vm = solder()
var input = stdin()

print("SOLDER. Type a line, or BYE to leave.")

if raw()
    // `tty_writer` and not `stdout`, because what the editor echoes has to appear as it is typed
    // rather than when a newline eventually flushes the buffer.
    var ed = editor(&input, tty_writer())

    session(vm, &ed)
    cooked()
else
    var cursor = console_lines(&input)

    session(vm, &cursor)
